# grem CLI

The grem CLI stamps deterministic project structure and emits agent-facing
prompts. It does not run an LLM or perform prompted lifecycle changes itself.

## Commands

- `grem init [TARGET]` scaffolds the declared folders and renders the declared
  template files into the target. Like `git init` it defaults to the bundled
  `python` template (override with `--template`/`-t`) and to the current
  directory, and names the project after the target folder — prompting when
  interactive, or take `--name`/`-n`. The name drives the template
  [[grem-config|variables]] (package name, proposal prefix) that are substituted
  during scaffolding and recorded in the config. It writes alongside unrelated
  files but refuses to overwrite existing ones, and refuses to run when the
  target already holds a `.grem` folder.
- `grem agent [PROJECT]` reads [[grem-config]] and prints the prompt for
  aligning configured agent instruction files. `grem instructions` remains as a
  hidden alias.
- `grem new --type TYPE --style STYLE PATH` validates a source file inside the
  project and prints the documentation-style prompt stored under
  `.grem/styles/<type>/<style>/prompt.md`, parameterized with that source path.
- `grem diff A B` validates two project-local scopes and prints the semantic
  comparison prompt for a numbered list of inconsistencies.
- `grem sync A B` validates the same kinds of scopes and prints the full plan →
  implement → test → document → diff loop.
- `grem upgrade [PROJECT]` requires a clean Git worktree, re-renders the newer
  template with the project's recorded variables, overlays only the
  template-owned control layer — every path matched by the project's
  [[templates|`.gremignore`]] is left untouched — and prints the target
  template's prompt for reviewing and merging the resulting unstaged diff.
  `--interactive` confirms before writing.

`init` and `upgrade` write project files. `diff`, `sync`, `agent`, and `new`
only produce text for a user to copy into an LLM agent such as Claude or Codex.
Every prompt command prints to stdout (pipe it, e.g. `grem agent | pbcopy`) and
accepts `--copy`/`-c` to place the prompt on the clipboard directly. A top-level
`--version`/`-v` prints the CLI version and exits.

Documentation styles are portable prompts under `.grem/styles/<type>/<style>/`,
each describing how to replicate one documentation style on any project. `new`
renders one against a chosen source file; the agent performs the actual work.

## Layers

The CLI layer loads and validates [[templates|template declarations]], config,
and SemVer values. Template content owns generated project knowledge and agent
behavior.

See [[grem-config]] and [[templates]].
